{
 "id": "M10",
 "kind": "echarts",
 "title": "The wider scale buys Haiku more cutoffs than it buys Sonnet",
 "section": "appendix",
 "order": 111,
 "caption": "The same operating points as the previous figure, cut by monitor instead of by batch. Each distinct honest score is one cutoff; x is the share of honest programs audited, y the backdoors caught. The dashed line is a 5% audit share. Use the dial to switch batch.",
 "notes": [
  "Counting cutoffs at or below a 5% audit share, the 0 to 100 prompt takes Haiku from 5 to 11 on the original 500 and from 2 to 5 on batch 2. Sonnet moves from 4 to 5 and from 0 to 2.",
  "Sonnet starts with less room to gain: it already catches most backdoors at its first cutoff, so extra cutoffs change where you can sit on the curve more than what you catch there.",
  "Same data as M09 and M11; three cuts of one comparison, kept so the report can use whichever reads best in place."
 ],
